WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.830
Panel session to be
moderated by Limor Peer.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.420
Limor Peer is the
Associate Director

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.630
for Research at the Institution
for Social and Policy Studies

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:28.920
at Yale University.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.830
She oversees research
infrastructure and process

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:35.790
at ISPA, including the Field
Experiment Initiative, which

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.620
encourages field
experimentation in

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:43.490
interdisciplinary collaboration
in the social sciences.

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.550
In this capacity,
she's led the creation

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.390
of a specialized research
data repository, the ISPA data

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.180
archive.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.900
Limor also serves as a
research data policy specialist

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.820
in the Office of
the Provost and is

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.140
working on campus wide efforts
relating to research data.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.900
And she has the
coolest first name.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.840
So I'd like to keep this
as informal as possible.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:12.700
This is the end of the day, and
I think we've all heard a lot

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.450
and have a lot to digest
and a lot to process.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.550
And by the way,
speaking of digestion,

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.490
if anyone wants to grab a
cup of coffee, that's fine.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.220
So I'm thinking of this
as just a conversation.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.630
I mean, from my point
of view, I would just

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.570
like to sit back and let these
guys talk among themselves

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:34.070
and just listen to that.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:37.990
So I think that's where
we're going to try to go.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.960
But I did want to say just maybe
a few things in the beginning.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.860
First, just to thank you all
for agreeing to do this today.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.230
I think that we're
very fortunate to have

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:51.100
this stellar panel of--

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.350
couldn't ask for a better set
of keynote speakers and folks

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.740
that can really make the
case for open science

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.520
that I think a lot of people
in this room are supporting,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.190
and that we are all
sharing the mission that we

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.250
need to spread the word
and get it out and make

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.790
real change fast.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:15.150
All of these speakers
also lead by example.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.470
And they've made open science
and research transparency

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:22.500
a priority alongside
their academic careers,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.400
which I find quite amazing.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.099
And I think they should
be saluted for that.

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:31.890
So we've heard about
the work of the Center

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.670
for Open Science, the
Open Science Framework,

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.950
the Reproducibility Project.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:41.610
We've heard from Erin
about her advocacy

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.580
work, her personal
pledge, and the work

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.512
she does to educate researchers.

00:02:46.512 --> 00:02:48.030
And we've heard from Harlan.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.360
Harlan's a leader in open
data on clinical trials

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.100
and the [INAUDIBLE] project
and some other things related

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.030
to that.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:59.100
We heard a lot about
the benefits of openness

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.980
to science as a whole.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.980
I really liked
Brian's, I think it

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.620
was yesterday's talk
at the Psychology

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.190
Department about your
motivation for starting

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.310
this whole thing was realizing
that there's a gap between how

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:17.670
we idealize science and how
we actually practice it,

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.390
and being a psychologist,
kind of realizing some

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.090
of the reasons for that gap
having to do with things

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:28.800
like biases and human
nature, but then coming up

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.340
with a way to kind of try
to tackle that and reduce

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.290
that gap.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:39.120
So benefits for science,
doing science better.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.670
Benefits for
scientists, so things

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:46.320
that will improve their career
and make it better for them

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.200
and better for
society and better

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.955
for research subjects,
which I think

00:03:50.955 --> 00:03:53.310
didn't quite come
up as much, but I

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.980
know that it's one of the
things that Harlan really

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.910
works on as well.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:01.920
So I guess I want to just
throw out a question to you

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.330
to bring it back to some of the
remarks that Steve Girvin made.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:07.676
Seems like a long time ago.

00:04:07.676 --> 00:04:10.170
It was this morning.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:14.940
So because open science,
really, it's an ecosystem.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.670
And we have a lot of players.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:18.004
We have researchers.

00:04:18.004 --> 00:04:20.459
We have academic societies.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:21.689
We have institutions.

00:04:21.689 --> 00:04:22.610
We have funders.

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.190
We have industry.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.850
We have journals,
we have libraries,

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:31.920
and we have technology and
other support services.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.220
I want to ask you to zoom in on
the institutions for a minute,

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:41.790
because if we're talking about
fast progress in this area,

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.970
maybe some of these stakeholders
have gone faster than others

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:47.370
if we charted them out.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.680
What are your thoughts
about where institutions

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.460
are in this sort of
progress, and especially

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:59.670
in light of some concerns
that I have coming from--

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.640
in the US, it's different,
for example, in the UK,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.610
where some of the
national policies

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.210
inform the research councils,
and then the research councils

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:12.120
tell the universities what to
do, when they have to do it.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.980
We don't really have that.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.080
And the recent
statements from the--

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.790
what is it, the Association
for American Universities,

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.430
might lead one to believe
that there is maybe a little

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:26.670
some resistance in how the
universities see themselves

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:30.900
complying with some of
the federal mandates

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.210
for opening up data.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:34.920
So that's my question.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.315
I'll let you go with that.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.460
You want me to jump in here?

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.170
Yeah, so I think--

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:51.487
I mean, it depends a lot what
institution you're looking at.

00:05:51.487 --> 00:05:53.320
So some institutions
have been great in kind

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.170
of pushing things forward.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:59.140
But I think what I would love
to see more from institutions

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:02.141
is a better match between
what they say they value

00:06:02.141 --> 00:06:03.640
and what they're
actually rewarding.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:07.000
So a lot of institutions have
these very explicit mission

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:10.540
statements that say, we
value communicating science.

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:13.000
We value reaching
out to the public.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.710
We are a public
institution that should

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:16.750
be interacting with citizens.

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:20.170
But at the time where we're
evaluating researchers

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:22.900
for hiring or
promotion and tenure,

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:26.140
we're not giving them credit
for communicating their results

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:29.500
to the public or
sharing their results.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.990
We're giving them more
points for locking it up

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.610
in a journal like
Nature and Science

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:36.190
than we are for dissemination.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:41.170
And so I would love to see more
initiatives from institutions

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.590
to have a better mismatch,
to reform their evaluations

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:46.630
and start valuing some
of these things more,

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:51.940
and really kind of meet
their mission statements,

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:55.427
because I think that this
is something that we can all

00:06:55.427 --> 00:06:57.760
agree, reaching out to the
public should be a good thing

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:00.419
and should be something that an
academic institution is doing.

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:02.710
But if we're just locking up
these results in journals,

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:05.410
and we're furthermore
writing it in language

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:07.780
that the public
can't understand,

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.120
that's not reaching out
to the public in any way.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.270
So I think a lot of institutions
have been very cautious,

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:15.910
and every department says
no, we're a unique snowflake

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:19.450
and we can't possibly have
kind of general recommendations

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:21.760
about how we could
reform evaluation.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:25.760
But I think we have to think
a lot more about those things.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.260
Yeah, I totally agree with
the aligning incentives

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:34.795
with what the goals are for
the institutional mission

00:07:34.795 --> 00:07:37.810
and scholarship,
as Erin describes.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:41.530
Another element of where
I think institutions still

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:45.550
have work to do on the
infrastructure side

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:49.450
is in finding ways
to actually integrate

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:54.010
the tools and services for
doing science more openly

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:56.720
with the researchers' workflow.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:58.510
So just as an
example, I have never

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:00.610
once used the
institutional repository

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:03.210
at University of Virginia.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:05.350
I'm probably among the
more committed people

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.890
to open science at the
University of Virginia,

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.900
and I've never used it because
it is not at all linked

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:12.970
to the workflows in my lab.

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:14.560
It never comes up
to think, oh, I

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.380
should deposit my articles in
our institutional repository.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:23.932
And I raised this with some
colleagues in the library,

00:08:23.932 --> 00:08:25.890
and they said, oh, that's
probably a good idea,

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:27.431
because they said
oh, and no one else

00:08:27.431 --> 00:08:29.910
uses our repository either.

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:32.549
And I mentioned that
the way that you

00:08:32.549 --> 00:08:35.280
can get me to use
the repository is,

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.770
each year I have to submit
an annual report of all

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.809
the research that I did.

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:41.740
Of course it's in
a Word document.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.940
And I cut and paste from
my vitae what work was,

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.620
which is a problem on its own.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:50.070
But if instead, during
that reporting process,

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.749
I just said, add your papers--

00:08:51.749 --> 00:08:54.040
it was a little web form and
I'm dropping my papers in,

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.520
and that automatically put
them into the institutional

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:59.810
repository-- then
everybody's papers on campus

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:01.830
would be in the
institutional repository.

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:02.370
Why?

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:05.380
Because we fill out that form
because we want our raise.

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:06.450
So it's in our workflow.

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:07.722
We have a reason to do it.

00:09:07.722 --> 00:09:09.180
And so you have to
think about what

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:12.030
is it that actually engages
people in the workflow

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.250
that they have in
order to get them

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.510
to use the services that the
organization's providing,

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:17.010
right?

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:20.776
Just saying do it
isn't sufficient.

00:09:20.776 --> 00:09:21.275
Yeah.

00:09:21.275 --> 00:09:23.792
So the University of
Liege, I think, in Belgium

00:09:23.792 --> 00:09:24.750
is actually doing this.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:26.291
So it's a requirement
now that if you

00:09:26.291 --> 00:09:28.400
want any product, any
research product considered

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.580
for promotion and tenure, it
has to be in their open access

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:33.170
institutional repository.

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:36.500
And there was this
great quote, actually,

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:39.230
just a few hours ago on
Twitter from the NIH Director

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:41.060
that said, if you
want to herd cats,

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:43.040
the best way is to
move the food bowl.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.580
And so this idea of being--
incentives are important.

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:49.160
So if you tell them your
raise depends on this,

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:51.062
your promotion and
tenure depends on this,

00:09:51.062 --> 00:09:52.020
they're going to do it.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.090
It's not just build
it and they will come.

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:57.960
They won't come unless you
give them a reason to come.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:02.740
And then there's another add-on
to that, which is recognition

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:07.180
that most researchers are not
concerned with open science.

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:09.790
And that's not
saying researchers

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:11.460
are opposed to open science.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.780
It's that the concern
of the researcher

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:15.690
is the problems that
they're trying to solve,

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:18.070
the questions that they're
investigating in the lab.

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:19.360
So I have a great test case.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:22.870
For all the things that
we do at the center,

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:25.210
my test case for whether
this is going to work at all

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:28.240
is whether my wife
gets interested in it.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:31.120
She runs a lab at UVA,
and most of the time

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:33.874
she has no interest at all in
what we're doing because she's

00:10:33.874 --> 00:10:35.290
trying to figure
out the cognitive

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:38.540
processes underlying anxiety
disorders and fixing that.

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:41.865
That the papers are available
open access, well, that's fine.

00:10:41.865 --> 00:10:43.990
Yeah, I hope other people
will be influenced by it.

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:46.930
But it's not what she
thinks about every day.

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:48.970
And so it's another
one of those occasions

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:51.490
of presuming that
someone-- she's

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:54.846
totally pro-open science
in the principles,

00:10:54.846 --> 00:10:56.470
but because she's
not thinking about it

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:59.680
in terms of the practices,
what we have to provide

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:02.282
is easy way to live
by those principles

00:11:02.282 --> 00:11:04.240
so that she can stay
focused on the things that

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:06.340
are the problems
she's trying to solve.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:10.000
And so any institutional
process has

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.910
to be attentive
to what's actually

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:15.760
driving the person's
behavior, and how do we just

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:16.780
become part of that?

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:18.430
Don't add onto the workflow.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:20.758
Integrate with their work flow.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:29.400
So these are
interesting suggestions.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:32.580
I guess-- and I'm looking for
you, Brian, as a psychologist,

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:37.000
is to think about
how do you create--

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:39.710
how do you make it
normative in a way that

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:44.240
isn't top down requirement,
but recognizing that your wife

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:50.070
or others will see this as not
just the right thing to do,

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.676
but actually making their
lives richer and better?

00:11:52.676 --> 00:11:55.050
And you start thinking about
what some of the pain points

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:55.110
are.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:56.710
I mean, some of the
pain points are,

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:58.920
you spend all this
time doing a paper.

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:00.180
It's a static paper.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:02.514
And I think a lot of them are
confetti papers, you know?

00:12:02.514 --> 00:12:04.721
They get a little attention
for a little bit of time,

00:12:04.721 --> 00:12:06.210
and then they sort of fall away.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.730
They don't nearly get the
eyeballs that they could get,

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:10.920
or aren't getting to
the right audiences that

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:13.860
might foster the
kind of commentary

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:17.310
and push you to better
work, and actually

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:19.500
help you see solutions.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:22.127
And in the beginning,
as investigators,

00:12:22.127 --> 00:12:23.960
I think we're taught,
hold everything tight.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:25.470
I'm the one with the ideas.

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:27.720
And it's only through
collaborative efforts

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:30.053
where you say, I never would
have come up with this idea

00:12:30.053 --> 00:12:32.310
if I hadn't had some sort
of resonance with somebody

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:35.220
else who was challenging
me, or that the group hadn't

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:36.850
been able to do that.

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:39.780
And so I guess I have
this wish to think

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:44.850
that we can create the tools
so that it gets out there,

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:48.030
and create fostering communities
and interactions that

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:49.080
never would have existed.

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:52.530
And then in some way
you're able to also--

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:55.110
it's not just about
citations, but it's actually

00:12:55.110 --> 00:13:00.440
about energy that's
generated around ideas.

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:02.310
Because otherwise
we're producing papers

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:04.840
and then they get
forgotten really quickly,

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:08.112
in part because they never get
to people who it might spark.

00:13:08.112 --> 00:13:10.070
The most excitement I
get is when someone says,

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:14.300
I read one of your papers, and
it led me to do something else.

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:16.430
And I just know
that a lot of people

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:18.320
are shielded with access.

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:21.470
If I think about the world, I
mean, certainly in our country,

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:22.850
if you're associated with a
university you're going to get

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:23.940
access to a lot of this stuff.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.180
But there are a lot of people
around the world who may not

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:28.013
be able to be exposed
to some of these ideas

00:13:28.013 --> 00:13:30.911
because of the way and
the costs and so forth.

00:13:30.911 --> 00:13:33.410
So we have to think about ways
of making these cases that it

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.510
creates a more
vibrant community,

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:37.079
more satisfying career and life.

00:13:37.079 --> 00:13:38.870
And that actually is
going to produce, even

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:40.490
for you, better science.

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:41.990
And I know that may
be a pipe dream,

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:43.790
but it's something
about feeding into that,

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:46.880
because I do believe if it feels
like one more darn thing I've

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:48.110
got to--

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:50.480
because it seems like
every year, as faculty,

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.610
we're asked to do more
compulsory training

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:53.960
on something.

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:54.950
The list is like this.

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:56.225
You've got to--
you know, I've got

00:13:56.225 --> 00:13:57.830
to take all these
online assessments.

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:02.487
And they don't feel energizing,
and I don't necessarily

00:14:02.487 --> 00:14:04.070
feel like it's
improving my competence

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:05.850
or making me better at anything.

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:07.694
I'm just someone's check box.

00:14:07.694 --> 00:14:10.235
And I think the degree to which
this becomes somebody's check

00:14:10.235 --> 00:14:12.590
box because we're telling
them it's making things

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:15.059
better rather than finding
out ways we can authentically

00:14:15.059 --> 00:14:17.600
do that-- and that's why I was
asking you when we met before,

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:19.760
how do we know the
science has gotten better?

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:23.180
You We need to be able to say,
this is a tool for the endgame.

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:26.120
The endgame is we're actually
better producing science.

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.600
Like if we go through all of
this and on the other side

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:32.570
we're just the same as we
are now, then all we've done

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:35.280
is create a lot of extra work.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:38.060
And so we've got to figure out
how does it actually enhance

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:42.590
the output, make it either
more efficient or more clever?

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:44.617
And that's I think
our central challenge,

00:14:44.617 --> 00:14:46.700
so that we're not just
sitting here telling people

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:48.075
it's the right
thing to do, we're

00:14:48.075 --> 00:14:49.670
going to put in
these regulations,

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:52.760
but that we're saying it's
a competitive advantage.

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:55.190
Last thing I'll
say about this is--

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:58.130
because I have this same
conversation in medicine

00:14:58.130 --> 00:14:59.787
around decision support tools.

00:14:59.787 --> 00:15:02.120
A lot of the docs are saying
it takes too long to engage

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:03.320
in decision support.

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:04.960
I'm smart enough, you know.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.309
I've memorized all this stuff.

00:15:06.309 --> 00:15:07.850
And what you want
to say is once they

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:09.350
can start looking
at their outcomes,

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:10.520
it's like the athletes.

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:12.600
You don't see any of
the athletes saying,

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:14.895
somebody's got a new
training regimen that ends up

00:15:14.895 --> 00:15:16.520
making them faster,
everyone else wants

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:17.990
to figure out what that is.

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:20.060
I mean, you like
that they stay legal.

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:21.890
But on the Olympic
side, you want

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:24.800
to give yourself every
advantage for performance.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:27.840
And what I want this is to
be performance-enhancing.

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:31.250
And if this becomes
performance-enhancing,

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:33.320
then things will
fall into place.

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:34.557
Then give credit for that.

00:15:34.557 --> 00:15:36.140
I mean, that's part
of it, performance

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:40.010
enhancing in what your job is as
a member of the community, too.

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:41.510
But if it's
performance-enhancing,

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:43.040
I think people will run to it.

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:46.010
If it seems like it's another
weight tied around my legs

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:48.920
that's making it hard for me to
swim, because I just don't have

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:51.253
enough time do my research
any more because I'm spending

00:15:51.253 --> 00:15:55.760
40% of my time complying
with all these mandates,

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:58.280
then I don't think it's
a long-term winner.

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:00.210
That's just my general
ideas about it.

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:00.710
Yeah.

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:04.730
These are real good
issues to identify.

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:10.010
And to take one step
back and then go into it,

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:13.430
the presumption of training--
and this is an academic

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:15.500
presumption, by and large--

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:17.130
is that we have to change--

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:19.370
we have to persuade people,
change their attitudes

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:23.100
and beliefs, and their
behavior will follow.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:26.094
Most of the evidence suggests
it's lot easier do the reverse,

00:16:26.094 --> 00:16:28.260
change the behavior, and
their attitudes and beliefs

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:29.940
will follow.

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:35.100
So set the conditions so that
the behaviors happen naturally.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:37.064
Not that I have to do
all this extra work

00:16:37.064 --> 00:16:38.730
or fill out all the
extra forms or think

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:39.750
about this new process.

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:44.610
But give me a tool that makes
it easy for me to do something

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:45.847
that I already want to do.

00:16:45.847 --> 00:16:47.430
I'm not persuading
you about anything.

00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:49.050
I already want to
collect my data.

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:50.120
All things being
equal, I want to do it.

00:16:50.120 --> 00:16:50.760
Right.

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:53.732
And then all of a sudden, it
also adds on this other thing.

00:16:53.732 --> 00:16:56.190
Oh, it turns out my research
is more reproducible because I

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:59.190
used this tool that was
an awesome analytics tool,

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:01.950
and all the code spit out, and
then it put it on this website.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:04.440
And then someone else clicks
go and all of the results

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:05.160
come out again.

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:06.339
Oh, that's cool.

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:07.680
Oh, actually, you know what?

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:08.730
I'm glad that's there.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:12.240
Oh, I believe in
reproducibility.

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:18.569
So the key for these is not,
let's build persuasion campaign

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:21.510
and convince people that despite
the incentives of the system,

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:24.119
despite the inconvenience,
despite all these forms

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:27.250
you have to fill out now,
it's a good thing to do.

00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:29.070
Instead, focus on the tools.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:32.400
How do we insert ourselves
into their workflows

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:36.535
so that they can just do it,
and then persuade themselves?

00:17:36.535 --> 00:17:38.910
And the other part of that is
that there isn't persuading

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:41.460
that's needed in the
ultimate endgame,

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:44.100
because you noted that
people are saying,

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:46.770
I need to hold this for myself.

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:48.060
I'm resisting.

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:51.060
But simultaneously, I
want to have influence.

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:53.310
Everybody wants to
have influence, right?

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:56.220
What is an academic's success?

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:57.990
It's reputation.

00:17:57.990 --> 00:18:00.600
If an academic went into
science to make money,

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:03.120
they made a bad career choice.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:06.070
They could make lots of money
in other ways more easily.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:10.140
The academic's real
thing is reputation.

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:11.410
How do we get reputation?

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:13.701
We have influence, we have
impact, people pay attention

00:18:13.701 --> 00:18:15.690
to what we're doing.

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:18.450
Being closed in different
parts of the process

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:20.710
interferes with that.

00:18:20.710 --> 00:18:22.980
But the reason that we feel
like we need to be closed

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:26.270
is because the entire
mindset is about,

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:28.440
I need to be close to
get the publication.

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:32.600
The publication is my means
to reputation enhancement.

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:36.390
And so breaking that system
is breaking the publication

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:39.730
as it is the bottleneck.

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:41.770
Everything is about
the publication,

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:43.780
and if you can break
that bottleneck,

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:46.750
then the I get impact, I get
attention, I get interest,

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:49.460
I get people talking to me
about stuff is now open.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:51.790
The entire process is
an opportunity for me

00:18:51.790 --> 00:18:54.460
to have impact, for other people
to pay attention to what I'm

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:55.914
doing, how I designed my study.

00:18:55.914 --> 00:18:58.330
Other people start to say, oh,
that's a really interesting

00:18:58.330 --> 00:18:58.829
question.

00:18:58.829 --> 00:18:59.820
Let me help.

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:03.160
Or oh, that question is now
inspiring me to do this thing.

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:04.870
And it's no longer
interfering with me

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:07.960
to have you start to take my
ideas earlier, because it's not

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:10.070
about the publication anymore.

00:19:10.070 --> 00:19:12.220
It's about me
influencing you, wherever

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:13.500
I am in the research cycle.

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:18.240
Where do you see this going?

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:24.120
Say that we're successful to
some extent and at some point,

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:28.890
and we have more and more
demand for open science

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:32.250
from our funders, and maybe
from researchers themselves,

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:33.030
and more supply.

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:36.120
We know that that's coming.

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:44.036
So what unforeseen
problem might be something

00:19:44.036 --> 00:19:45.660
that we're not thinking
about right now

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:48.660
that might come up that
has to do with open science

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:51.930
that we-- because it seems that
the way we're thinking about it

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:54.610
is it solves a lot of problems,
and it's a good thing.

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:57.300
But are there some
problems that it

00:19:57.300 --> 00:20:01.060
could create that we
should maybe think about?

00:20:06.391 --> 00:20:06.890
So yeah.

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:09.320
I think-- I mean, we've
touched on some of the issues,

00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:10.280
privacy issues.

00:20:10.280 --> 00:20:13.700
There might be times where
disclosing certain information

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:15.710
is not good for the patient.

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:18.590
So I think those are kind
of the more obvious cases.

00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:20.930
I think one that I hear a
lot, and I don't necessarily

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:25.010
have a good answer for, is
simply too much information.

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:27.680
So if you're trying to keep
up with a certain field,

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:29.840
there are already
too many articles

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:33.290
being published for me
to keep up with that.

00:20:33.290 --> 00:20:37.080
If you're going to add pre-print
literature to that, and data

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:41.000
sets, and protocols, and all
kinds of interim research

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:44.300
products that we think it's a
good thing to release those,

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:46.610
there's a filtering issue.

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:49.310
So I can't handle,
I can't process

00:20:49.310 --> 00:20:51.350
all of that information.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:54.260
How are we going to put some
types of filters in place

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:56.210
that allow us to pick
out the stuff that's

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:59.700
most relevant to us
and go with that?

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:03.910
So I think that's not yet an
issue we've entirely solved.

00:21:03.910 --> 00:21:06.230
I don't think that should--

00:21:06.230 --> 00:21:10.590
I don't think-- sorry,
yeah, yeah, I know.

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:15.080
So I don't think that should
prevent us from going forward.

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:17.570
But I think it's
something that we're

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:19.493
going to have to think
of going forward.

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:25.760
You know, I mean, I see day
where we're solving problems

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:26.260
together.

00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:28.660
Nobel Prizes don't go out
to three or few people,

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:31.690
but they recognize the team
nature of this science,

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:37.270
and that we're each
trying to push forward.

00:21:37.270 --> 00:21:41.529
But I do think we come back to
this issue about in the end,

00:21:41.529 --> 00:21:43.570
there is some curation
that's going to be needed,

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:45.090
so how does that curation occur?

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:48.250
I was saying to
Brian, in my field

00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:50.860
there are very small
but highly vocal

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:55.380
people around particular
issues that represent outliers.

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:57.790
Now not saying their voice
doesn't deserve to be heard,

00:21:57.790 --> 00:22:01.770
but for example, people
are anti-vaccine.

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:06.080
And they could threaten
the public's health.

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:08.889
And do they have an opportunity
to express their opinion?

00:22:08.889 --> 00:22:11.180
Sure, but if but if you've
got a small number of people

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:14.510
pelting the comments in
disproportionate fashion,

00:22:14.510 --> 00:22:17.300
you can have a
misappreciation of what

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:18.500
the community is thinking.

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:21.260
So how you curate this,
what represents a danger

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:24.930
if the information is
getting out in a certain way,

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:27.170
how do you how to
control what really

00:22:27.170 --> 00:22:29.600
should be reasonable discourse?

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:32.360
That is, one side does think
the other side is crazy.

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:34.235
The other side thinks
the other side's crazy.

00:22:34.235 --> 00:22:35.930
But actually both of them have--

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:38.637
we don't know who's right
yet, versus one where there's

00:22:38.637 --> 00:22:40.970
a group that's truly an outlier
opinion that potentially

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:44.540
could threaten the public's
health by saying something that

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:45.327
is--

00:22:45.327 --> 00:22:47.660
everyone should be taking
cyanide tablets, I don't know.

00:22:47.660 --> 00:22:51.110
You know, something--
this is a cure for cancer,

00:22:51.110 --> 00:22:52.910
like they did for
apricot pits for awhile.

00:22:52.910 --> 00:22:55.554
But the question is
how to curate that.

00:22:55.554 --> 00:22:57.470
And I think the issue
of credit does come down

00:22:57.470 --> 00:22:59.127
because we can't ignore this.

00:22:59.127 --> 00:23:00.210
So we've got a university.

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:02.380
Someone's is trying
to build a career.

00:23:02.380 --> 00:23:05.640
I want to give her
credit for the influence.

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:08.030
I want to give her special
credit if she produces data

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:09.265
that other people are using.

00:23:09.265 --> 00:23:11.390
I want to consider that
even better than a citation

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:15.050
because that study never would
have been possible if you

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:16.760
hadn't produced that data.

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:18.830
But the institutions
are slow to move,

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:21.950
and we're slow to understand
how to measure that influence.

00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:24.980
So when a junior person is
coming up doing good work,

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:29.190
being extraordinarily generous,
adopting this kind of approach,

00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:30.530
I worry.

00:23:30.530 --> 00:23:33.530
How are we going to make sure
that we can keep up with that

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:35.960
and not lose that
talent because there

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:38.480
are early adopters of what is
really the right thing to do?

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:40.980
And I think those are issues
that we're going to figure out.

00:23:43.270 --> 00:23:44.320
I agree with all those.

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:46.870
They cover a lot of
the broad domain.

00:23:46.870 --> 00:23:48.980
I think the key--

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:52.630
for me, the umbrella issue
is that the biggest risk

00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:56.530
is being comfortable
with the status quo.

00:23:56.530 --> 00:23:59.980
Whatever change we make to
now it's open, now it's this,

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:01.510
we're done.

00:24:01.510 --> 00:24:03.770
That is the wrong method.

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:06.670
We have to have, I think, in
order for this to be effective,

00:24:06.670 --> 00:24:08.920
an actual discipline
of meta science

00:24:08.920 --> 00:24:13.490
emerge that is truly the
interdisciplinary discipline,

00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:17.740
which is a constant evaluation
and iteration of refinement

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:22.120
of the process of scholarly
communication and research.

00:24:22.120 --> 00:24:25.060
Without that, we
will not identify

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:27.010
unintended consequences,
and we will

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:29.470
fail to improve the ones
where things are actually

00:24:29.470 --> 00:24:32.950
not operating as efficiently
or effectively as they could.

00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:34.450
Beautifully said.

00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:37.360
One challenge that comes to
my mind when I hear that is--

00:24:37.360 --> 00:24:40.270
because I've been around
librarians for a long time--

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:43.660
is the preservation
of these materials

00:24:43.660 --> 00:24:46.930
and the usage of them
in the long term.

00:24:46.930 --> 00:24:49.796
And what are your
thoughts on that?

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:56.080
Who is responsible for that?

00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:58.157
Who is responsible
for preserving?

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:04.450
I don't know.

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:06.580
And you know, producing
multiple terabytes

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:10.360
a second is mind blowing for
thinking about preservation

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:11.890
of data.

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:15.370
But there are lots of
interesting solutions

00:25:15.370 --> 00:25:17.840
that are emerging and
will continue to emerge.

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:22.990
And so to me, the problem right
now is to get to the point

00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:25.090
where we have a problem
with actually preserving

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:27.381
the data we need, because
that would be a great problem

00:25:27.381 --> 00:25:28.660
to have.

00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:33.130
So one solution that we've been
exploring with some prototypes

00:25:33.130 --> 00:25:36.670
is using torrents models
for data preservation.

00:25:36.670 --> 00:25:39.520
So torrents are usually
used for teenagers

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:41.860
to share movies when
they're not supposed to.

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:43.750
But they can be used
for good purposes, which

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:46.930
is every machine has
a bit of space that's

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:49.770
dedicated to hosting data.

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:52.220
And so I'd donate a few
gigabytes of my hard drive

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:53.970
and you'd donate a few
gigabytes of yours.

00:25:53.970 --> 00:25:56.560
It distributes it, and it is
a great preservation device

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:59.790
because you can get many, many
copies in many different places

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:02.490
and track [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:05.700
For preserving the
origins of things,

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:08.610
the blockchain has lots
of interesting potentials

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:11.750
for provenance and registration
and things like that.

00:26:11.750 --> 00:26:13.700
So there's
technology's constantly

00:26:13.700 --> 00:26:16.310
emerging that can help
solve some of the problems.

00:26:16.310 --> 00:26:17.860
And then what data
to get rid of.

00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:21.050
And then museums have a whole
history of thinking about what

00:26:21.050 --> 00:26:22.700
is the decision
process for deciding

00:26:22.700 --> 00:26:25.490
what to preserve because
we can't preserve it all?

00:26:25.490 --> 00:26:28.130
So I worry less about
that in the sense

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:30.200
that, yeah, when those
are real problems,

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:31.970
we figure out how to
solve those problems.

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:34.310
CERN is a great
example of having

00:26:34.310 --> 00:26:37.230
lots of real principled
decisions about when

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:38.960
and how to get rid of data.

00:26:38.960 --> 00:26:41.521
I'd just drop it because it's
not what needs to be preserved.

00:26:44.680 --> 00:26:47.050
Let's open it up to everyone.

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:56.500
My question is about
pre-prints, because it

00:26:56.500 --> 00:27:00.010
sounds like there are more and
more possibilities to submit

00:27:00.010 --> 00:27:01.630
a pre-print.

00:27:01.630 --> 00:27:07.720
And I think for most of us, the
traditional peer review process

00:27:07.720 --> 00:27:09.250
is really painful.

00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:12.580
And I always think
the reviewers feel

00:27:12.580 --> 00:27:15.130
forced to come up with
really crazy ideas

00:27:15.130 --> 00:27:16.780
or be really creative
in suggesting

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:20.970
impossible experiments.

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:23.350
Sometimes it's to really
improve their publications.

00:27:23.350 --> 00:27:25.600
Sometimes it's not.

00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:27.550
At least that's my opinion.

00:27:27.550 --> 00:27:31.960
So back to pre-prints,
is there any evidence

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:34.810
that a submitting a
pre-print would really

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:38.410
accelerate the time of
until the actual publication

00:27:38.410 --> 00:27:39.640
of a manuscript?

00:27:39.640 --> 00:27:42.160
And the second question
is if there is the fear

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:44.560
that reviewers will
also look at some

00:27:44.560 --> 00:27:49.390
of the suggestions published
in these pre-print services

00:27:49.390 --> 00:27:51.010
and just come up
with even more ideas

00:27:51.010 --> 00:27:54.490
so that you end up
doing more experiments.

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:56.760
At the end the application
will be very long,

00:27:56.760 --> 00:27:58.677
will probably have
a lot of authors,

00:27:58.677 --> 00:28:00.760
and will be much better
than the first manuscript.

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:03.610
But at the end, it will be
very time-consuming and also

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:05.745
very costly to finish it.

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:10.300
Yeah.

00:28:10.300 --> 00:28:11.590
So it's a good question.

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:13.964
I don't know if there's ever
been a controlled study that

00:28:13.964 --> 00:28:18.200
looked at whether manuscripts
that had been published

00:28:18.200 --> 00:28:22.000
on a pre-print server go
through the peer review process

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:25.240
faster, for example, than
ones that were submitted

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:26.860
to that type of platform.

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:30.580
My anecdotal experience
is just that having

00:28:30.580 --> 00:28:33.940
those eyes on the paper and
getting a lot of community

00:28:33.940 --> 00:28:36.910
feedback before I
submit to peer review

00:28:36.910 --> 00:28:39.660
has helped me clean
up a lot of issues,

00:28:39.660 --> 00:28:43.210
tackle kind of the biggest
issues in my papers.

00:28:43.210 --> 00:28:45.220
And I felt like the
peer review process

00:28:45.220 --> 00:28:48.520
after that, then, has
gone very smoothly.

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:51.390
So again, that that's
not a controlled study.

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:52.780
It's anec-data.

00:28:52.780 --> 00:28:56.590
But for me, that's my
personal experience.

00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:59.740
So this recent paper that we
wrote together, so we actually

00:28:59.740 --> 00:29:01.780
opened that up on Google
Docs and invited people

00:29:01.780 --> 00:29:03.940
to come in and give comments.

00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:09.160
And that allowed us to kind
of identify and really tackle

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:12.970
major issues, and then the peer
review process at the journal

00:29:12.970 --> 00:29:16.070
went fairly quickly.

00:29:16.070 --> 00:29:18.830
I don't know if there's
ever been a systematic study

00:29:18.830 --> 00:29:20.120
on pre-prints.

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:21.314
I can't think of one.

00:29:21.314 --> 00:29:22.730
But that would be
an awesome thing

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:25.610
to do for exactly the
reasons you identify,

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:27.620
because that would
be interesting.

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:31.330
You can come up with plausible
stories in both directions.

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:33.620
The positive story
and accelerating,

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:36.650
but the interference
story, as you describe it.

00:29:36.650 --> 00:29:38.580
So that's a study worth doing.

00:29:38.580 --> 00:29:41.060
So the only studies I am
aware of on pre-prints

00:29:41.060 --> 00:29:44.090
is some of the citation
studies surrounding

00:29:44.090 --> 00:29:48.620
pre-prints, so in physics
and mathematics especially,

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:51.710
the articles that were
published on archive

00:29:51.710 --> 00:29:55.850
prior to journal
submission and publication.

00:29:55.850 --> 00:29:57.590
Obviously they started
to get citations

00:29:57.590 --> 00:29:59.420
sooner because they
were available sooner,

00:29:59.420 --> 00:30:01.580
but then they maintained
the citation advantage

00:30:01.580 --> 00:30:03.930
for months or years afterwards.

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:08.540
So that's an obvious visibility
benefit for researchers.

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:12.589
Whether there are costs
that kind of outweigh that,

00:30:12.589 --> 00:30:13.130
I'm not sure.

00:30:13.130 --> 00:30:15.171
And I'm not sure those
things have been measured.

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:26.560
I have both a comment
and a question.

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:31.210
Comment is about getting
more and more open data

00:30:31.210 --> 00:30:36.810
and open applications
that we can use.

00:30:36.810 --> 00:30:40.600
I overlook one big
group of people

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:43.480
next to going into
society, which

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:48.910
are the groups that are not
in an Ivy League university.

00:30:48.910 --> 00:30:53.410
I know that I'm very privileged
with my whole education system.

00:30:53.410 --> 00:30:58.630
And visiting
universities or colleges

00:30:58.630 --> 00:31:02.620
that want to use and want
to partake in hypothesis

00:31:02.620 --> 00:31:08.830
and research, and I'm using, for
example, Matlab or Mathematica,

00:31:08.830 --> 00:31:11.350
and they don't have that.

00:31:11.350 --> 00:31:15.130
That's why I think it's
also a very important thing

00:31:15.130 --> 00:31:18.830
to emphasize that we as
researchers, from such

00:31:18.830 --> 00:31:22.030
a privileged university, should
also emphasize that we have

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:24.550
our colleagues at other
universities that really

00:31:24.550 --> 00:31:25.510
benefit from it.

00:31:25.510 --> 00:31:28.780
But we need to be the
drivers, because to be fair,

00:31:28.780 --> 00:31:30.020
they do look up to us.

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:32.800
So if we are the first
one to implement that,

00:31:32.800 --> 00:31:35.050
they definitely will follow.

00:31:35.050 --> 00:31:37.180
That was my comment.

00:31:37.180 --> 00:31:41.830
My question is more about we
already were discussing that

00:31:41.830 --> 00:31:44.830
and were going into
that direction in a way

00:31:44.830 --> 00:31:50.170
that what we see now, and what's
also now in public debated,

00:31:50.170 --> 00:31:55.420
is when everything is open
and we get facts and news,

00:31:55.420 --> 00:32:01.450
how are we going to deal
with that social way of who

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:03.580
is going to pick what
is right information,

00:32:03.580 --> 00:32:06.234
and what is the right
kind of data or research

00:32:06.234 --> 00:32:07.150
we're going to follow?

00:32:07.150 --> 00:32:10.825
Because we do see that now
in public news sharing,

00:32:10.825 --> 00:32:11.700
these kind of things.

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:13.880
But that probably is
also a consequence

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:18.140
of having all this open data and
all this kind of open research

00:32:18.140 --> 00:32:22.220
debating, because if we don't
have a curated list there

00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:29.300
anymore, then we base them
why certain people get popular

00:32:29.300 --> 00:32:31.760
on prejudices of others.

00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:34.050
So my question is,
how do you want

00:32:34.050 --> 00:32:36.870
to tackle that instead
of curating or maybe

00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:39.215
not creating open access?

00:32:43.700 --> 00:32:45.100
Both good comments.

00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:50.276
I'll address the first, and
you guys can answer the second.

00:32:50.276 --> 00:32:52.850
I think something that's
often overlooked--

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:54.920
and we did in our
discussions by and large

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:56.390
here, although not completely.

00:32:56.390 --> 00:32:58.430
I think Erin made
some good points--

00:32:58.430 --> 00:33:01.280
is that open science isn't
just about transparency.

00:33:01.280 --> 00:33:04.520
Openness also means
inclusivity, right along

00:33:04.520 --> 00:33:08.810
the point that you made, which
is, it really breaks down

00:33:08.810 --> 00:33:14.040
barriers that are because of
unequal resource distribution.

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:17.580
So one of the questions
earlier was about doing studies

00:33:17.580 --> 00:33:19.100
in multiple samples.

00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:20.880
Samples vary, especially
human samples,

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:24.740
and so we need to look at
variation in those effects.

00:33:24.740 --> 00:33:27.590
We have organized
many labs projects

00:33:27.590 --> 00:33:30.950
where we take the same protocol
and invite any lab that

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:34.130
wants to run it to run the exact
same protocol to investigate

00:33:34.130 --> 00:33:35.660
that very question.

00:33:35.660 --> 00:33:39.110
And those kinds of studies have
been very popular among groups

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:42.920
that are in institutions that
don't have dedicated research

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:43.700
resources.

00:33:43.700 --> 00:33:45.590
And the faculty
are spending most

00:33:45.590 --> 00:33:47.780
of their time teaching
rather than having time

00:33:47.780 --> 00:33:49.070
to do research.

00:33:49.070 --> 00:33:51.650
And the reason is that they
have passion and interest

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:53.810
and expertise in doing research.

00:33:53.810 --> 00:33:55.880
They just don't have
resources or grad students

00:33:55.880 --> 00:33:58.160
or other things to do
a generative research

00:33:58.160 --> 00:34:01.320
program starting from scratch
and building up the ideas

00:34:01.320 --> 00:34:02.330
and blah blah blah.

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:06.350
But to be able to participate,
if another group is saying OK,

00:34:06.350 --> 00:34:09.080
we'll organize these materials,
we'll organize the protocols,

00:34:09.080 --> 00:34:13.310
and then your role is like this.

00:34:13.310 --> 00:34:16.460
But it's a real role,
it is a meaningful role

00:34:16.460 --> 00:34:19.190
as dramatically expanded
inclusion in that.

00:34:19.190 --> 00:34:20.719
Same thing with open data.

00:34:20.719 --> 00:34:25.159
I gave a talk at University of
Belgrade a couple of years ago.

00:34:25.159 --> 00:34:27.199
And when I was talking
to the graduate students,

00:34:27.199 --> 00:34:30.860
they were all doing research
in my subdomain, implicit bias.

00:34:30.860 --> 00:34:33.440
So why are you guys all
studying implicit bias?

00:34:33.440 --> 00:34:38.820
And they said, well, all
the people in your subfield

00:34:38.820 --> 00:34:41.429
put their papers online
and so we can get them.

00:34:41.429 --> 00:34:44.159
So that's why we
study implicit bias.

00:34:44.159 --> 00:34:46.500
Like, oh my gosh, OK.

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:47.070
Wow.

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:51.073
That makes me feel like I'm
going to continue doing that.

00:34:51.073 --> 00:34:53.489
And why aren't other people
making their papers available?

00:34:53.489 --> 00:34:55.739
So these inclusivity
aspects, I think,

00:34:55.739 --> 00:34:57.300
are easy to under appreciate.

00:34:57.300 --> 00:34:58.800
When you have
resources already, you

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:00.258
don't realize what
the barriers are

00:35:00.258 --> 00:35:01.730
to getting access to things.

00:35:04.555 --> 00:35:06.680
I mean, first of all, I
just wanted to second that.

00:35:06.680 --> 00:35:08.930
So I work in Mexico,
and I'm fairly

00:35:08.930 --> 00:35:10.820
fortunate to work at
[INAUDIBLE], which

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:13.687
has pretty good resources
and fairly good access

00:35:13.687 --> 00:35:14.270
to literature.

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:16.310
But I did previously
work in an institution

00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:19.160
that had almost no
access to the literature,

00:35:19.160 --> 00:35:21.820
and saw what that
did on a daily basis

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:25.010
to our ability to
teach students,

00:35:25.010 --> 00:35:27.260
our ability to do research.

00:35:27.260 --> 00:35:29.720
And so when you do see
that on a daily basis,

00:35:29.720 --> 00:35:32.510
it really hits home,
the importance of what

00:35:32.510 --> 00:35:34.430
we're talking about here.

00:35:34.430 --> 00:35:36.760
And with respect to
open data specifically,

00:35:36.760 --> 00:35:39.050
you hear a lot of concerns.

00:35:39.050 --> 00:35:43.130
In Mexico we have limited
finances for research.

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:46.866
So every little piece of data
that I can get is so crucial,

00:35:46.866 --> 00:35:48.740
and I have to suck all
the juice out of that.

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:50.690
So why would I share
it with somebody?

00:35:50.690 --> 00:35:52.760
On the other hand,
if other researchers

00:35:52.760 --> 00:35:54.980
are sharing their
data, I might be

00:35:54.980 --> 00:35:57.620
able to get access to data
sets that I couldn't possibly

00:35:57.620 --> 00:35:59.450
collect where I
am because there's

00:35:59.450 --> 00:36:01.190
no way I'm going
to have the funds

00:36:01.190 --> 00:36:02.450
for that type of equipment.

00:36:02.450 --> 00:36:03.470
So neural recording.

00:36:03.470 --> 00:36:06.370
I'm not going to have
fancy neural recording.

00:36:06.370 --> 00:36:08.870
I'm trying to scrape together
a patch clamp break right now,

00:36:08.870 --> 00:36:11.210
and we're pulling
things out of closets.

00:36:11.210 --> 00:36:14.180
And that's the kind of level
that we're at right now.

00:36:14.180 --> 00:36:16.580
So there's that flip side to it.

00:36:16.580 --> 00:36:19.580
I could get a very significant
benefit from people

00:36:19.580 --> 00:36:22.220
outside opening up
their data, but there

00:36:22.220 --> 00:36:24.260
has to be some kind of
reciprocation. that's

00:36:24.260 --> 00:36:26.630
the problem.

00:36:26.630 --> 00:36:29.180
I'm kind of stalling here
because your second question is

00:36:29.180 --> 00:36:31.280
really not an easy
one to answer, which

00:36:31.280 --> 00:36:34.190
is why I was bringing it
up as a potential concern

00:36:34.190 --> 00:36:37.160
going forward, this
filtering issue.

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:39.460
How do you decide
what's important

00:36:39.460 --> 00:36:41.960
and what's not important, and
how do you kind of put weights

00:36:41.960 --> 00:36:42.459
to that?

00:36:42.459 --> 00:36:44.710
So a lot of people--

00:36:44.710 --> 00:36:47.090
previously-- well,
number of citations.

00:36:47.090 --> 00:36:49.310
Well, that's a
fairly poor metric.

00:36:49.310 --> 00:36:50.780
Journal impact
factor even worse,

00:36:50.780 --> 00:36:52.280
because it's a
journal level metric,

00:36:52.280 --> 00:36:54.740
it doesn't say anything about
the actual individual piece

00:36:54.740 --> 00:36:56.300
of work.

00:36:56.300 --> 00:36:59.210
And so there have been a lot
of efforts around alt metrics,

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:04.007
so can you kind of get a bigger
picture of how much impact

00:37:04.007 --> 00:37:05.090
a piece of work is having?

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:08.900
So Tweets and Facebook
and news media coverage

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:09.860
and things like that.

00:37:09.860 --> 00:37:15.140
And try to get quantitative
and qualitative idea

00:37:15.140 --> 00:37:17.390
of how big an impact
a piece of work

00:37:17.390 --> 00:37:20.270
is having, and then have some
type of ranking based on that.

00:37:20.270 --> 00:37:22.520
And a lot of people get very
concerned that, well, you

00:37:22.520 --> 00:37:24.035
can game these metrics.

00:37:24.035 --> 00:37:26.660
Well, you can game
citation counts, too.

00:37:26.660 --> 00:37:29.180
And citation counts,
to a certain extent,

00:37:29.180 --> 00:37:31.010
are also popularity contests.

00:37:31.010 --> 00:37:33.590
So labs get popular,
those names get big,

00:37:33.590 --> 00:37:35.690
and you cite that
one just because it

00:37:35.690 --> 00:37:37.670
has the big name
versus somebody who

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:40.140
might have made a similar
discovery around the same time.

00:37:40.140 --> 00:37:43.340
So I think there's
not an easy answer.

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:47.060
But there's a lot of
people, like Impact Story,

00:37:47.060 --> 00:37:50.600
who are working on how
we might get a broader

00:37:50.600 --> 00:37:53.860
picture of an impact of a
piece of work, and then work on

00:37:53.860 --> 00:37:56.199
and ranking and filtering.

00:37:56.199 --> 00:37:57.740
We had somebody who
presented earlier

00:37:57.740 --> 00:38:00.620
on this idea of up
voting or down voting,

00:38:00.620 --> 00:38:03.590
and then commenters can sort
of build their reputation

00:38:03.590 --> 00:38:05.660
over time, a Reddit-type
system that I

00:38:05.660 --> 00:38:06.800
think is also interesting.

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:08.467
So I don't think
we have a solution,

00:38:08.467 --> 00:38:10.550
but I there are a lot of
very smart people working

00:38:10.550 --> 00:38:11.299
on these problems.

00:38:11.299 --> 00:38:13.910
So I hope they will have
a better answer for you

00:38:13.910 --> 00:38:17.482
than I do in couple
of years from now.

00:38:17.482 --> 00:38:19.940
I'm kind of like Brian was on
that other issue, which is, I

00:38:19.940 --> 00:38:21.231
think it's going to get solved.

00:38:21.231 --> 00:38:22.779
Look, I think all
the consumer sites

00:38:22.779 --> 00:38:24.320
are kind of working
on this, figuring

00:38:24.320 --> 00:38:26.342
how do you manage the gaming?

00:38:26.342 --> 00:38:27.830
And I think what
we have to realize

00:38:27.830 --> 00:38:32.080
is we have to get away from
an omnibus kind of score.

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:35.380
But it's like, who
is it influencing?

00:38:35.380 --> 00:38:36.300
Where is it good for?

00:38:36.300 --> 00:38:38.856
Even for you, you're
interested in the curation.

00:38:38.856 --> 00:38:40.480
You may want to be
interested in scores

00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:43.480
from certain kinds of people or
certain ways it's being done.

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:45.436
I see this as moving fast.

00:38:45.436 --> 00:38:47.810
People are going to come up
with a lot of different ways.

00:38:47.810 --> 00:38:49.410
You'll have, I think,
a lot of options.

00:38:49.410 --> 00:38:50.743
The question will be the demand.

00:38:50.743 --> 00:38:54.450
So as this starts
spreading and we

00:38:54.450 --> 00:38:58.720
start being inundated by
the amount of possibilities,

00:38:58.720 --> 00:39:00.510
there's going to demand
to try to come up

00:39:00.510 --> 00:39:03.900
with ways to customize
what you want,

00:39:03.900 --> 00:39:06.330
and also for you to understand
who's making use of it

00:39:06.330 --> 00:39:07.121
and how it's going.

00:39:07.121 --> 00:39:10.830
I think it could become
more democratic in a way

00:39:10.830 --> 00:39:12.270
because you're right.

00:39:12.270 --> 00:39:13.937
And there's plenty
of studies showing

00:39:13.937 --> 00:39:16.020
this whole thing about
citation is totally flawed.

00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:18.120
I mean, certain things
get hot, and they

00:39:18.120 --> 00:39:20.169
actually-- in my field
people aren't even

00:39:20.169 --> 00:39:21.460
reading the literature anymore.

00:39:21.460 --> 00:39:23.479
So there are papers
that either we publish,

00:39:23.479 --> 00:39:25.520
other people publish that
we think are important,

00:39:25.520 --> 00:39:26.830
and they're totally
ignored in the papers.

00:39:26.830 --> 00:39:28.830
They're just sort of
like doing a quick look.

00:39:28.830 --> 00:39:30.540
Maybe they're looking at the
last paper that was published

00:39:30.540 --> 00:39:32.831
and pulling out some stuff
from their reference section

00:39:32.831 --> 00:39:34.250
and just moving forward.

00:39:34.250 --> 00:39:37.260
And I'm seeing, actually,
less thorough understanding

00:39:37.260 --> 00:39:40.580
of what the literature is, a
great need to improve that.

00:39:40.580 --> 00:39:43.500
And I know adding more is not
going to be the solution to it.

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:45.580
But I think the status
quo is a big problem.

00:39:45.580 --> 00:39:47.520
And so the popularity
contest here

00:39:47.520 --> 00:39:49.350
isn't necessarily merit-based.

00:39:49.350 --> 00:39:51.340
It's sort of
name-based, and so we're

00:39:51.340 --> 00:39:53.340
going to have to figure
out what it is that it's

00:39:53.340 --> 00:39:55.200
going to move us forward.

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:55.700
Yeah.

00:39:55.700 --> 00:39:57.440
We are overwhelmed
with information

00:39:57.440 --> 00:39:59.190
in every part of our lives.

00:39:59.190 --> 00:40:01.490
And there's a lot of
commercial organizations

00:40:01.490 --> 00:40:05.030
whose business model depends
on good filtering of that

00:40:05.030 --> 00:40:06.560
for their success.

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:10.220
When I go to New York, I use
Yelp, and most of the time

00:40:10.220 --> 00:40:11.960
the restaurant I
go to is like, oh,

00:40:11.960 --> 00:40:13.370
yeah, that was a good choice.

00:40:13.370 --> 00:40:15.262
So then I continue to use Yelp.

00:40:15.262 --> 00:40:16.370
What you really want to know is
how much the person liked it.

00:40:16.370 --> 00:40:18.495
Like you, I mean, I feel
like that on Flixster

00:40:18.495 --> 00:40:19.370
and all those things.

00:40:19.370 --> 00:40:21.720
I mean, they they need to get
better too, because Rotten

00:40:21.720 --> 00:40:23.210
Tomatoes, I don't care what
the average Rotten Tomatoes

00:40:23.210 --> 00:40:23.709
score is.

00:40:23.709 --> 00:40:26.570
I want to know people who
like the movies that I like.

00:40:26.570 --> 00:40:29.090
They haven't begun to customize
that yet, but they will.

00:40:29.090 --> 00:40:31.220
And in five years no one
is ever going to see,

00:40:31.220 --> 00:40:32.750
well, this is the average
Rotten Tomatoes score.

00:40:32.750 --> 00:40:34.500
It's really popular
because all the people

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:36.110
that rated it like
that kind of movie.

00:40:36.110 --> 00:40:37.860
They all went to it
and they all rated it.

00:40:37.860 --> 00:40:42.380
And I think in our field
it will be the same thing.

00:40:42.380 --> 00:40:44.420
And I think what we'll
have to be careful about

00:40:44.420 --> 00:40:47.450
is not getting in a bubble where
all of a sudden our interests--

00:40:47.450 --> 00:40:49.490
and they just feed us stuff.

00:40:49.490 --> 00:40:51.200
So we have to be
able to say, we also

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:54.550
want the random one coming our
way that somehow tickles you.

00:40:54.550 --> 00:40:58.100
So all this stuff, though,
I think will get worked out.

00:40:58.100 --> 00:41:00.380
So I think something else
we have to be vigilant about

00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:04.300
is who controls these tools, who
controls this infrastructure.

00:41:04.300 --> 00:41:05.490
I think we should.

00:41:05.490 --> 00:41:06.480
Well, right.

00:41:06.480 --> 00:41:07.340
Right.

00:41:07.340 --> 00:41:08.480
The two of us.

00:41:08.480 --> 00:41:10.500
Oh, no, no.

00:41:10.500 --> 00:41:12.890
But you mentioned
commercial entities.

00:41:12.890 --> 00:41:14.810
So there are
publishers right now

00:41:14.810 --> 00:41:16.760
that are trying to
latch onto the idea

00:41:16.760 --> 00:41:20.090
that open is becoming
popular and use

00:41:20.090 --> 00:41:23.510
that to gain financially.

00:41:23.510 --> 00:41:25.760
There was a particular
publisher that

00:41:25.760 --> 00:41:27.860
was getting into the
metrics game in terms

00:41:27.860 --> 00:41:30.170
of research or evaluation.

00:41:30.170 --> 00:41:33.620
And the problem is that
because these are closed tools

00:41:33.620 --> 00:41:35.960
and closed systems, for
example, we don't know,

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:38.322
is that publisher weighting
their journals heavier

00:41:38.322 --> 00:41:39.530
than other people's journals?

00:41:39.530 --> 00:41:41.690
We don't know because we
can't see inside that.

00:41:41.690 --> 00:41:43.520
Thomson Reuters has
been controlling

00:41:43.520 --> 00:41:46.104
impact factor for years.

00:41:46.104 --> 00:41:47.420
We should boycott all of that.

00:41:47.420 --> 00:41:49.767
Yeah, we should, and there
are lots of people who have.

00:41:49.767 --> 00:41:51.225
I mean, there's
the San Francisco--

00:41:51.225 --> 00:41:53.141
A place like Yale would
take the lead on that.

00:41:53.141 --> 00:41:54.317
So this would be fantastic.

00:41:54.317 --> 00:41:56.900
So how many people have heard
of the San Francisco Declaration

00:41:56.900 --> 00:41:59.660
on Research Assessment, SF DORA?

00:41:59.660 --> 00:42:03.350
So this is a statement that
says we are essentially

00:42:03.350 --> 00:42:04.740
boycotting impact factor.

00:42:04.740 --> 00:42:08.030
We're not going to use it in
any hiring, promotion, tenure,

00:42:08.030 --> 00:42:09.320
evaluation.

00:42:09.320 --> 00:42:11.900
And it's got over 12,000
individual signatories

00:42:11.900 --> 00:42:13.380
and I think 900 organizations.

00:42:13.380 --> 00:42:15.610
But if places like Yale
then sign this and then

00:42:15.610 --> 00:42:17.860
act on it, because it's not
just going and signing it.

00:42:17.860 --> 00:42:19.700
It's signing it and
then saying we're

00:42:19.700 --> 00:42:21.680
going to remove the
mention of impact factor

00:42:21.680 --> 00:42:23.780
in all of our tenure and
promotion guidelines.

00:42:23.780 --> 00:42:26.540
We're going to explicitly
say that committees are not

00:42:26.540 --> 00:42:29.330
supposed to look at this
when evaluating candidates

00:42:29.330 --> 00:42:32.280
for new positions,
that type of thing.

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:36.900
So yes, we should boycott
these things as individuals,

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:39.320
as institutions, and
be very vigilant who's

00:42:39.320 --> 00:42:41.480
controlling this
infrastructure, and are

00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:44.134
we able to see inside it
and see what's going on?

00:42:44.134 --> 00:42:46.550
So I think that's why it's so
important that, for example,

00:42:46.550 --> 00:42:49.175
you guys are building tools that
are open source and people can

00:42:49.175 --> 00:42:51.720
see what's under the hood, and
people can grab it and modify.

00:42:51.720 --> 00:42:53.053
What should we do with Facebook?

00:42:53.053 --> 00:42:54.252
I'm not going there.

00:42:54.252 --> 00:42:56.490
You guys can touch that one.

00:42:56.490 --> 00:42:59.550
Brian and I were talking
about this, though.

00:42:59.550 --> 00:43:01.050
I totally agree
with you about that.

00:43:01.050 --> 00:43:02.883
I was just raising this
other issue, though,

00:43:02.883 --> 00:43:05.220
about these fake news
groups and all this.

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:07.470
This is where I think how
this stuff gets-- there

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:10.110
will be need for some curation.

00:43:10.110 --> 00:43:12.760
And so how that gets
done, who does it,

00:43:12.760 --> 00:43:15.300
and the degree of transparency
around how it's done.

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:19.950
All of these are going to be
important facets, I think.

00:43:19.950 --> 00:43:22.498
I'll just say I think that could
be a role for the university

00:43:22.498 --> 00:43:24.988
because it knows
its researchers,

00:43:24.988 --> 00:43:27.079
it knows the domains,
and that they

00:43:27.079 --> 00:43:29.470
could be a gatekeeper in that
way, as well as a leader.

00:43:29.470 --> 00:43:31.960
That's another good role
for the institution.

00:43:31.960 --> 00:43:34.450
Karen, do we have
time for one more?

00:43:34.450 --> 00:43:36.960
Yeah.

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:38.739
You have the final word.

00:43:38.739 --> 00:43:40.280
Well, this is really
just a follow-up

00:43:40.280 --> 00:43:41.530
of the curation question.

00:43:41.530 --> 00:43:43.405
Just do you think
artificial intelligence has

00:43:43.405 --> 00:43:45.205
any role in that
in terms of, kind

00:43:45.205 --> 00:43:48.070
of like, equivalent to
decision support or Watson

00:43:48.070 --> 00:43:52.985
trying to make judgments about
what's valid and not valid?

00:43:56.670 --> 00:43:57.170
Yeah.

00:43:57.170 --> 00:43:58.370
And how far it can go.

00:43:58.370 --> 00:43:59.240
Who knows?

00:43:59.240 --> 00:44:04.030
If it can start writing my
papers for me, all the better.

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:06.500
But there are lots of
interesting advances

00:44:06.500 --> 00:44:08.090
that are looking
for relationships

00:44:08.090 --> 00:44:09.230
that are hard to detect.

00:44:09.230 --> 00:44:12.140
The Allen Institute has
some interesting new tools

00:44:12.140 --> 00:44:15.790
along those lines about
the scientific literature.

00:44:15.790 --> 00:44:18.700
So yeah, I think there's
lots of potential there.

00:44:18.700 --> 00:44:21.380
Yeah, so we were just discussing
text and data mining yesterday

00:44:21.380 --> 00:44:25.130
at the poster session, and kind
of how quickly those tools are

00:44:25.130 --> 00:44:25.630
advancing.

00:44:25.630 --> 00:44:29.059
So the Content Mine, for
example, is one project.

00:44:29.059 --> 00:44:30.850
So their goal is to
extract a million facts

00:44:30.850 --> 00:44:31.683
from the literature.

00:44:31.683 --> 00:44:33.250
Well, I can't do that by hand.

00:44:33.250 --> 00:44:35.770
But the amazing text
and data mining tools

00:44:35.770 --> 00:44:37.780
that they're developing can.

00:44:37.780 --> 00:44:41.110
And also I was looking
into a project with them

00:44:41.110 --> 00:44:42.160
on an image mining.

00:44:42.160 --> 00:44:44.860
So we have all these neural
traces in the literature.

00:44:44.860 --> 00:44:47.560
And right now, if I want to
get an idea of the diversity

00:44:47.560 --> 00:44:50.470
of that, I have to go
one paper at a time

00:44:50.470 --> 00:44:51.730
and look at those figures.

00:44:51.730 --> 00:44:54.640
And it's just a ridiculous
task to do manually,

00:44:54.640 --> 00:44:56.170
especially if now
I have to consider

00:44:56.170 --> 00:44:58.753
all the pre-print literature and
all the published literature,

00:44:58.753 --> 00:45:00.280
and maybe open datasets as well.

00:45:00.280 --> 00:45:04.210
But as we're developing new
text and data mining tools--

00:45:04.210 --> 00:45:07.450
not me, them-- you can
extract this information.

00:45:07.450 --> 00:45:08.890
So they had this
really nice kind

00:45:08.890 --> 00:45:11.620
of proof of concept
demonstration

00:45:11.620 --> 00:45:14.860
where they were able
to pull out the data,

00:45:14.860 --> 00:45:17.530
recreate the data from a
figure, and put it back

00:45:17.530 --> 00:45:20.410
into a spreadsheet that now you
could analyze and play with.

00:45:20.410 --> 00:45:22.960
And so the potential
for that, I think,

00:45:22.960 --> 00:45:25.750
would be incredible if we
could do large scale analyses

00:45:25.750 --> 00:45:28.210
and comparisons
across neural traces,

00:45:28.210 --> 00:45:31.150
or across other types
of chemical structures,

00:45:31.150 --> 00:45:34.604
or things like that
we can't do manually.

00:45:34.604 --> 00:45:36.520
I'd just say I don't
think there's any choice.

00:45:36.520 --> 00:45:37.450
It's going to be that way.

00:45:37.450 --> 00:45:39.033
But there'll be
performance enhancers.

00:45:39.033 --> 00:45:40.590
I don't think they'll replace.

00:45:40.590 --> 00:45:42.730
There'll be guides, different
tools people can use.

00:45:42.730 --> 00:45:44.022
I'm not an AI expert myself.

00:45:44.022 --> 00:45:45.980
But what I've read is
there's some concern even

00:45:45.980 --> 00:45:48.670
among those experts about
the black box effect,

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:50.170
because algorithms
have gotten so

00:45:50.170 --> 00:45:52.090
complex and sophisticated
that it becomes

00:45:52.090 --> 00:45:55.150
difficult to go back and
validate what comes out,

00:45:55.150 --> 00:45:57.450
the output of this at times.

00:46:02.884 --> 00:46:04.860
OK?

00:46:04.860 --> 00:46:06.836
Thank you all.

00:46:06.836 --> 00:46:17.740
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:17.740 --> 00:46:19.826
So in addition to
thanking the keynotes,

00:46:19.826 --> 00:46:21.700
I actually wanted to
give a round of applause

00:46:21.700 --> 00:46:23.116
for all the speakers
we had today.

00:46:23.116 --> 00:46:25.370
It was just some
fantastic talks.

